    const cliCommands = response.commands.filter((cmd) => cmd.cliCompatible !== false)
    const sorted = [...CLI_ONLY_COMMANDS, ...sortCommandsWorkflowsFirst(cliCommands)]
    setAllCommands(sorted)
  })
```

Because that fetch runs in the hook body, it runs on every render. Each resolve then calls `setAllCommands(sorted)` with a fresh array identity, which triggers another render, which kicks another fetch, and so on.

Why this matters in practice:
- typing in the input causes frequent re-renders already
- this now adds repeated slash-command fetches on top of that
- races can produce stale command lists (earlier request resolves after later request)
- this can hammer controller/state access and make slash UX feel unstable

The previous code path in `ChatView` used `useEffect` for command loading, so this is a behavior regression, not just a refactor.

Suggested shape:

```ts
const [allCommands, setAllCommands] = useState<SlashCommandInfo[]>([])

useEffect(() => {
  if (!ctrl) {
    setAllCommands([])
    return
  }

  let cancelled = false

  getAvailableSlashCommands(ctrl, EmptyRequest.create())
    .then((response) => {
      if (cancelled) return
      const cliCommands = response.commands.filter((cmd) => cmd.cliCompatible !== false)
      setAllCommands([...CLI_ONLY_COMMANDS, ...sortCommandsWorkflowsFirst(cliCommands)])
    })
    .catch(() => {
      if (!cancelled) setAllCommands([])
    })

  return () => {
    cancelled = true
  }
}, [ctrl])
```

I would also consider memoizing `slashInfo` and filtered commands exactly once per render from stable inputs, which you are mostly already doing.

Test coverage suggestion:
- unit test hook behavior with mocked `getAvailableSlashCommands`
- assert it is called once per controller instance, not once per render
- assert no state update happens after unmount

